 {
  "id": "2120718224031715706",
  "title": "Bioluminescent jellyfish swarm in Three.js",
  "title_zh": "Three.js 深海发光水母群",
  "category": "3d-scene",
  "author": {
   "handle": "superalesha",
   "name": "Alexey Fateev"
  },
  "postUrl": "https://x.com/superalesha/status/2120718224031715706",
  "createdAt": "2026-11-12T03:20:00.000Z",
  "durationMs": 37248,
  "mp4": "https://www.sandbase.ai/lab/media/x/qwen4-max/2120718224031715706/video.mp4",
  "promptShared": true,
  "promptText": "Qwen4-Max made a deep ocean full of glowing jellyfish in Three.js. Same prompt in 2 harnesses.\n\nTop: DeepSeek Harness. Bottom: Qwen Code. Both on xhigh.\n\nThe Qwen Code version flashed black boxes for 1 frame at a time, the DSH one blew the screen white on click. 1 bug report each and both fixed it, so the model can debug its own shaders when you point at the problem.\n\nTokens, DSH: 11.22M input, 10.80M cached, 110K output\nTokens, Qwen Code: 10.84M input, 10.18M cached, 121K output\n\nPrompt:\nCreate a deep-ocean scene in Three.js, in a single HTML file: a swarm of bioluminescent jellyfish drifting through dark blue water. Each jellyfish has a soft, pulsing bell made with a vertex shader and long tentacles simulated with verlet physics that trail and sway as it swims. Add glowing light patterns that ripple across the bells, volumetric light shafts from the surface far above, drifting marine snow particles, depth fog and bloom. Moving the mouse creates a gentle current that pushes the jellyfish and bends their tentacles; clicking makes the nearest ones flash and dart away. Keep it smooth at 60 fps. When you are done, open the page in the browser, look at screenshots and fix what looks wrong.",
  "featured": null,
  "poster": "https://www.sandbase.ai/lab/media/x/qwen4-max/2120718224031715706/poster.jpg",
  "labUrl": "https://www.sandbase.ai/lab/ai-videos/qwen4-max/2120718224031715706"
 },
